window.docContents[11].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Main-Objects-and-Hypotheses":{"contents":"An abstract weak problem is a bounded bilinear form  together with a bounded\nlinear load , and a solution satisfies\n\n\n\nThe concrete Poisson solver assumes a bounded domain in positive dimension.\nIts Dirichlet form  is built from weak gradients; Poincaré's inequality\npromotes gradient control to coercivity on . The solver is a bounded\nmap of the square-integrable load, satisfies an a priori norm estimate, and is\ncharacterized by both the variational equation and minimization of the\nDirichlet energy.\n\nVariable principal coefficients enter first as bounded operators on the gradient\ncoordinates, with pointwise  coefficient fields arriving through\nmultiplication operators. Almost-everywhere uniform ellipticity gives an\nintegrated lower bound by the Dirichlet form. Combining this bound with the\nbounded-domain Poincaré inequality gives the coercivity Lax–Milgram needs.\n\nLower-order terms can destroy positivity. What survives is a Gårding estimate,\nwhich says the form is coercive up to an  error, and adding a large enough\nmultiple of the pivot inner product turns that into a shifted coercive problem\nwith a unique weak solution and a quantitative solver bound.\n\nAlmost-everywhere uniform ellipticity and integrated uniform ellipticity are different predicates: the\nfirst describes a coefficient field almost everywhere, the second is an\nintegrated operator inequality, and passing from the first to the second is a\ntheorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems","header":"5.4.2.2. Main Objects and Hypotheses","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Main-Objects-and-Hypotheses"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure--Solenoidal-Spaces-and-Weak-Pressure--Convection-on-the-constrained-space":{"contents":"For smooth fields on a domain where the boundary flux vanishes, the model\nconvection form is\n\n\n\nThe product rule gives\n.\nIntegration by parts turns this into\n. For example, compact support\nremoves the boundary term. Setting  explains the energy cancellation:\nincompressible transport redistributes energy without creating it. The\nabstract theorem takes this skew identity as a hypothesis; in a concrete\nSobolev space it comes from justifying the integrals and the boundary step.\n\nLet  be a continuous trilinear convection\nform. Assume the integration-by-parts skew identity for solenoidal transport\nfields: for  and ,\n\n\n\nRestriction of  to  is again a continuous trilinear form.\nEvery ambient product boundpasses to the restricted form with the same constant. Skew-symmetry for\nsolenoidal transport fields becomes global skew-symmetry on ,\nand thereforeThe nonlinear energy cancellation holds without choosing a particular domain\nor a Navier–Stokes solution predicate.\n\nCompose each slot of the ambient form with the continuous inclusion\n. Subtype norms equal ambient norms, so the\nproduct estimate is unchanged. The first restricted argument is solenoidal,\nso the assumed integration-by-parts skew identity applies; diagonal\nskew-symmetry then gives the cubic cancellation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Solenoidal Spaces, Compactness, and Pressure\u0009Solenoidal Spaces and Weak Pressure","header":"8.3.1.2. Convection on the constrained space","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure--Solenoidal-Spaces-and-Weak-Pressure--Convection-on-the-constrained-space"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Exact-Planar-Flows":{"contents":"In the two exact flows treated here, the nonlinear term becomes a pressure\ngradient or balances diffusion exactly. Each is stated in coordinates: the velocity, the pressure,\nand the divergence and momentum residuals are explicit functions of \nor , written with one-variable derivatives, and the cas tactic\nchecks that the residuals vanish. Both proofs are compiled examples in\nPDE.Examples.Research and appear here as worked examples. Neither flow has\nfinite energy on the whole plane, so they illustrate the equations and not the\nLeray–Hopf theory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow","header":"8.5.2. Exact Planar Flows","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Exact-Planar-Flows"},"/Introduction/#pde-introduction":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nImagine a rod with a warm middle and both ends held at zero temperature.\nHeat flows toward the cold ends, and the temperature falls. The heat equation\ndescribes that evolution. An explicit formula gives one solution; an energy\nestimate proves uniqueness and controls changes in the initial temperature.\n\nOn a rod with its ends held at zero temperature, a sine-shaped profile decays\nwithout changing shape:\n\nHere time and length have been scaled so that the heat equation is\n. Differentiating once in time and twice in space gives\n\nSo : the profile satisfies the heat equation. It also vanishes at\nboth ends of the rod, and at time zero it is . These are separate\nfacts that together identify the solution and its data.\n\nThe first calculation checks .\n\n\n\nThere is a reason this calculation closes so neatly. The second derivative\nof  is , so substituting  reduces the\nPDE to . A spatial eigenfunction has turned the evolution into\nan ordinary differential equation. For the higher mode , with\npositive integer , the same calculation gives\n. Finer oscillations decay faster.\n\nThe same initial shape behaves differently on a vibrating string. With zero\ninitial velocity, the wave equation gives .\nBoth ends stay fixed in each problem. Compare their evolution below.\n\n\n\nFor the heat mode, try . Its amplitude at time  equals the amplitude\nof the first mode at time . At the same amplitude, the extra oscillations\nmake the spatial gradient larger and increase the rate of heat dissipation. For the wave\nmode, displacement and velocity exchange energy as the string vibrates.\n\nThe calculation also exposes what has not yet been answered. It gives one\nsolution; uniqueness needs an argument that applies to every solution in a\nspecified class. Replacing the initial sine by a rough temperature profile\nrequires a construction that survives limits. Replacing the rod by all of\nspace leads to the Gaussian kernel, whose initial point mass is understood\nthrough convergence against test functions rather than pointwise substitution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"1. Introduction","id":"/Introduction/#pde-introduction"}});